WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:06.290
Fundamentally, at the core of
everything, you want an interaction to be

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.019
very natural.

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:12.000
Whether the design is superb, and it fits
exactly the way that you would anticipate

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.929
it, it just feels natural.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:16.670
And that’s exactly where our field comes
into play.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.040
How do you make that thing natural?

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.140
It’s not just a design perspective, but
it’s also, what is the content that you’re

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.140
showing?

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.140
How are you going there?

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.580
So, it’s transforming that experience, instead
of just making it better.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.570
-You’re listening to the Microsoft
Research Podcast, a show that brings you closer

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.940
to the cutting edge of technology research,
and the scientists behind it.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.580
I’m your host, Gretchen Huizinga.

00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:46.260
Big data is a big deal, and if you follow
the popular technical press, you’ll have

00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:53.909
heard all the metaphors: data is the new oil,
the new bacon, the new currency, the new electricity.

00:00:53.909 --> 00:00:55.870
It’s even been called the new black.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.960
While data may not actually be any of these
things, we can say this: in today’s networked

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.480
world, data is increasingly valuable, and
it is essential to research, both basic and

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.480
applied.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.729
Today, we welcome a special guest to the podcast.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:16.850
Dr. Igor Perisic is the Vice President of
Engineering and Chief Data Officer at LinkedIn,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.250
the social network for business and employment.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:25.810
Today, Dr. Perisic talks about the key attributes
of a data scientist, how AI and machine learning

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:31.210
are helping personalize member experiences,
why we should all be big open source fans,

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.000
and how LinkedIn is partnering with other
researchers through their innovative Economic

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.119
Graph program to “create economic opportunity
for every member of the global workforce.”

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:50.390
That and much more on this episode of the
Microsoft Research Podcast.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:59.180
Igor Perisic, it’s great to have you
here on the podcast joining us via Skype from

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.900
Mountainview. Welcome.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.040
-Thank you very much.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:02.840
-Yeah.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.440
Igor, you’re the VP of Engineering at LinkedIn,
and you’re also the Chief Data Officer.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:12.670
So, in broad strokes, give us an overview
of what you do, and define your responsibilities

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:13.670
in each role.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.190
-So, I’m an engineer.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.250
I build things.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:22.090
I build the data systems, the infrastructure,
that powers the back end of LinkedIn, and

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.290
the offline systems upon which we can manipulate
our data.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:31.120
And I’m a scientist in the sense that I
build also the algorithms and the personalizations

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.540
that we can provide to our members using those
systems, to make the experience better for members.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.720
So, the Chief Data Officer is bridging these
two, and has a little bit of a component around

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.430
policy, in the sense of making sure that on
one side, the security, engineering and legal

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:52.350
are talking together to address the problems
that can arise from there.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:57.830
-LinkedIn has been both a thought leader
and early adopter in the field of data science.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:02.230
Talk a little bit about that field in general,
and, more specifically, its history and current

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.230
role at LinkedIn.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.049
-Well, so history.

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:06.280
we can go far, far back.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.180
I mean, the terms “data” and “science”
have been defined, let’s say, in the English

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.660
dictionary, somewhere around the 17th century.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:19.230
But fundamentally, I think it had another
change of direction somewhere within the last

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.800
ten years.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.940
It became somewhere around 2007 or 8-ish,
which we worked at LinkedIn, and D.J.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.810
Patil, who was my product counterpart at that
time, somewhat coined a term within the, let’s

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.019
say, more recent generation of it, which was
more about an individual who would really

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:42.920
do five things: who could hack code, create
code, who can reason around data and see the

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.190
product that it has inherently within it,
and hack the product for it.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.180
An individual who was really good about machine
learning, so it can actually create those

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.269
algorithms.

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:54.120
An individual that could communicate.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.120
So, you can see the story.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.450
You can, maybe, write it up.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.939
But you can communicate that to somebody else,
and not just towards somebody who’s very

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:03.470
knowledgeable about it.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.280
And a great engineer who would build infrastructure
to it.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.980
Because you’ve got to remember that 5 or
6, 7 years ago, the infrastructure wasn’t there.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.880
So, you have to build everything.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:17.519
Today, I see it migrating back more towards
the ability to create the story, the ability

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:22.000
to see the pattern through the data, and then
how it can actually help a product.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.509
-And the term “data scientist” is
also a very connotative term right now.

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:31.139
-Yeah, I think originally it
was because we created something that was

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:32.139
different.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:33.770
And we gave it those two terms.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:38.820
Then, later on, as with everything, something
becomes very hot and sexy, as a career.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.660
So, then everything becomes “data science.”

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.660
-Right.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.670
-So, we do the same thing as
we call it, a relevance engineer is the same

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:51.280
thing as we call it, data scientist, or that
we would call it just researchers, research

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.130
engineers, data engineers.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.870
They all migrate around the same topics, so
certainly.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:01.390
-Now, you just mentioned a term, “relevance,”
which is a term I’ve seen along and around

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:02.970
your website.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:03.970
What does “relevance” mean?

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.550
-Actually, that’s a good question.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:11.130
It’s certainly something that’s very specific
at LinkedIn, where we move towards being much

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:16.220
more data-informed in the way that we build
products and we take decisions through time.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.039
And then bringing all this optimization, techniques…
we didn’t want to call it like optimizers,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.100
optimize this, optimize that… but making
things more relevant.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.840
And that’s where we sort of keyed around
the term “relevance,” as the science to

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.949
make things more relevant, which is fundamentally
to make something more personal to an individual.

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:34.949
-I love that.

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:37.440
And I think that resonates with a lot of people.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.600
So, let’s move over to the mission of LinkedIn
and it’s, to quote, “giving economic opportunity

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.610
to every member of the global workforce.”

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:51.050
So, tell us what role data science and research
play in making that mission happen.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.789
-Well, we do have a fundamental
role at the core, because when you create

00:05:54.789 --> 00:05:59.530
economic opportunity for every member of the
global workforce, you’re creating that economic

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:01.490
opportunity for an individual.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.090
And that individual is always unique.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.389
Certainly, from, let’s say, a statistical
perspective, it’s part of a bigger bucket.

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:11.720
But at the core, that opportunity is unique
for that individual.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:16.020
And in that situation, it means that you need
to actually build up your products or build

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.600
up your experiences to that individual, and
to tailor it to him or her.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.760
And that’s exactly where our field comes
into play.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:29.620
You can create broad-scope experiences, but
when it starts getting into this personalization

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:34.780
aspect, that’s where, exactly where machine
learning comes in, or AI.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:41.280
[music]

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.000
-So, in a recent post on your engineering
blog last year, it was called “Celebrating

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.140
Research Excellence in LinkedIn.”

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.530
You gave a shout out to your researchers for
3 things.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.090
One of course is making your products better.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:57.460
But you also referred to contributing to the
open source community, and producing world-class

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.160
research, peer-reviewed research.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.300
So, why is the work of your researchers important
to the scientific community at large, and

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.220
vice versa, I would say?

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.240
-[laughs] I grew up with an ideal
of science.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.770
And I, um, there was something that resonated
to me from a very, very long time.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:16.770
And I don't know why.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.900
You know, you grow up and there’s some sentences
that stick with you.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.800
And one that really stick with me from very
early on was, “Standing on the shoulder

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.160
of the giants.”

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.129
And the reason for that is I could see the
broad scientific community as just one community.

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:36.320
And however great you were, you were standing
on somebody’s shoulders.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:38.379
And sooner or later, somebody would stand
on your shoulders.

00:07:38.379 --> 00:07:40.830
So, it was never something that it was done
for yourself.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:45.009
It was something that was done for the greater
community and leveraging all that knowledge

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:46.200
and moving it forward.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.520
So, then of course, within that scope, contributing
back is very critical and very important on

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.360
two things: one, well, if you have the next
generation, the next individuals; and also,

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.460
a measure of quality.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.520
You may think that what you’ve done is great,
but it may be completely wrong.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.139
So, once you expose it, it’s when you get
that feedback that you can actually learn.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:12.120
Being right or wrong is not necessarily fundamentally
the most valuable thing, compared to just

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.400
the experience of learning.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:17.750
-So, there’s been always a bit of tension
in the research community between proponents

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:21.530
of applied research and basic research, 
especially in industry.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:27.139
What’s your philosophy about the role of
basic research in industry, where the ROI

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:31.960
may be years out instead of immediate, and
what role does basic research play at LinkedIn,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:35.020
a company whose product research is basically
embedded?

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:39.450
-So, I’m a big proponent of
research, whether it’s basic or applied.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.310
And it’s all a matter of context.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:46.380
I believe that if you only limit yourself
to just applied research, you tend to sort

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.280
of focus into just one area, and just dig
in more and more and more and more.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.860
And you’re preventing yourself from actually
seeing this paradigm shift that can occur.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:00.840
Basic research has this goodness, or this
overall perspective of, that there’s something

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.980
that’s extremely challenging, and there’s
something that advances our understanding,

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.610
and in itself is valuable.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:12.250
The application will follow or will not follow,
but it will spark ideas.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.560
Within the industry, it’s actually really
hard to do just basic research.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.350
It’s just a matter of whether you have the
ability to do so.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:22.820
In the end, the majority of companies are,
need to generate a certain amount of revenue,

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.020
so you have to be able to actually balance
the two.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:30.960
There, Microsoft, I think it was very, very
good at balancing the basic as well as applied

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:31.970
research.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:36.610
At LinkedIn when we started, we were a startup,
so you can’t really do basic research or

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:41.160
fundamental research and go to, let’s say,
the CEO at that time, in your group of 5 or

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:46.060
6, and work on something and, “Don’t worry
about it, in 200 years, it will make a difference.”

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:50.000
Because you don’t know whether the company
is going to be there two or three years down

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.000
the road.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.420
So, you have to play within in, but still
have a very good perspective and overall view

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:58.850
that those two worlds are sort of intertwined.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:02.510
You can see the work that Microsoft has done
through the years.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:07.750
Fundamentally, you can say, “Well, at which
point of time does, let’s say, um, quantum

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:09.640
computing become basic to applied?”

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.980
Well, at the beginning, it was certainly basic.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.070
And today, it’s almost applied.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.900
Although, the window is very far ahead.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:16.900
-Yeah.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.380
-It’s not happening tomorrow,
but it is happening.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:25.060
-So, let’s talk about research cycle
for a second, specifically at LinkedIn.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.940
What does it look like time-wise and outcome-wise
for you, and how is it similar and different

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.330
from maybe some other things?

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.940
Because you have a really unique approach
to research at LinkedIn.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.170
-Yeah.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.000
Our cycles are much shorter.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.460
Like, for example, the thing that we’ve
published, I made it sure that, very early

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:45.650
on, that publications would be about things
that we’ve developed and shipped on the

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:50.610
site and had affected our members’ experiences,
compared to things that we’ve developed

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:55.440
in an offline environment on some subqueries
and whatnot, and it moved some metric.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.620
It had to have gone all the way in.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:03.790
So, in this case, our cycles are different
in a sense that the end value is around whether

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:06.200
it actually went all the way down to the site.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.940
Now, tying into what you said, there’s also
another cycle.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:12.720
It’s, how long do you research before you
make a difference?

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:18.860
And there, our window at LinkedIn is I think
most often, except for infrastructure, where

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:22.880
it takes time to develop, it’s probably
maybe 1 or 2 years out.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.260
So, it’s certainly not Microsoft Research.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:26.290
-Right.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:31.160
So, what’s the relationship between the
researchers at, say, Microsoft Research now,

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:33.740
and LinkedIn, now that you’re sort of part
of the same family?

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.030
-Well, it’s in multiple dimensions.

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:43.580
One is, in some places, we were, let’s say
for example, the neural networks, and using

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:48.840
CNNs and GPUs, Microsoft was far ahead of
the curve, and then we’re benefiting from

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.890
the learnings from it.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:54.910
For example, how to set up the topology of
our clusters, how do we set up the topology

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:57.970
of our, let’s say, rankers for it.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:02.680
So, then we’re benefiting from knowledge
that somebody else has done, if you want.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.310
And there’s also another side as well.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:09.050
We have a different perspective at times,
and then it’s just sharing it, as I mentioned

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:14.720
a couple of times here, that science advances,
or research advances, when you bridge those

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.970
different types of connections with different
types of fields.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.050
So, we bring another perspective.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:25.150
We’ve been interacting very closely with
the New England team, who has been our, let’s

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:28.470
say, partner and front door to the rest of
Microsoft Research.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.790
As you can expect, there’s lots of interest
for Microsoft Research.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:36.010
It’s good that it’s a little bit centralized,
so we can actually navigate around all those

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.560
expectations and demands.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:45.220
[music]

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:49.780
-So, you talk about the importance of
what you call “conversations” with your members.

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:54.180
And there are multiple, multi-level conversations
going on at any given time.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:58.390
So, from a technical point of view, how do
you stay on top of those and make sense of

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:00.320
those conversations in a meaningful way?

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.140
-I think it’s a new perspective
that I’ve looked at it, and you tend to

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.050
think that once you’ve found out this new
paradigm, the way that you’re going to communicate

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:11.720
about it, it makes sense, and you wonder why
you didn’t see it before.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:16.180
And probably in 5 to 10 years, I would look
at myself and what a complete idiot I was.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.610
But that’s the nature of things.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:24.150
Trying to find a model that can help you reason
about a problem that you have.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:30.240
In this situation, it’s about conversations
in a sense that very early on, we try to do

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:34.300
things with our members, 
get our members to do things.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:40.060
And in this case, a conversation is really
an informal chat, if you want, between two

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:43.120
individuals, and it’s between with let’s
say LinkedIn and the member.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:47.100
And early one, what we’ve done is say, 
well, we just blasted things.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:50.130
We just sent you emails like there’s no
tomorrow.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.240
We did very good optimization saying that,
well, the more email you get, the more likely

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:55.240
you’re going to do something.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:56.380
Which is probably wrong.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:57.460
-Not me.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.520
-Uh. My point.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:03.480
But apparently, somebody had done a study,
and that seemed to be good.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:09.080
Of course, when you look at high-level statistics,
it seems to be fine.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.770
But the conversation was more like me screaming
at you.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:15.120
And then when you start shifting by saying,
well, there’s a lot of conversation that

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.440
we can have, so which one should I communicate
to you, like, right now?

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:25.140
And today, or in the recent past, we’ve
moved more into thinking about, well, this

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.620
conversation doesn’t, don’t stop just
with the action.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:31.540
That there’s some sense of, what are you
doing at LinkedIn, and what do you want to

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:32.580
achieve at LinkedIn?

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:36.950
And those goals don’t stop tomorrow, don’t
stop on the click, don’t stop on the view,

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:39.080
don’t stop on the share.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:40.930
They go for a longer period of time.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:45.810
And the problem then starts shifting, because
very often the techniques would be, I need

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:51.890
to find, let’s say, a way to – use some
different techniques – to figure out what

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:54.070
is your likelihood to act on something?

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:55.770
It’s a probability.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.260
And in that case, whether you do logistic
regressions, whether you do different types

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:04.630
of all the way up to uh, neural nets, you
come up with a number.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:06.920
And that number, and then you optimize around
it.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:09.630
And it’s usually just about that activity.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:15.030
Now, if you view it in the context of a conversation,
that activity doesn’t stop right there.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:16.030
It continues.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.030
We’re having a dialogue.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:18.790
It’s going to go on for a little while.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.190
You can’t just optimize for the next step.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.010
So how do you go in and out of that?

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.010
-Right.

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.650
-And I felt that it’s the right
time to think about it.

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:33.580
Although, we had thought about it already
some years ago, simply because of the shift

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:38.980
that we’re seeing nowadays with more and
more of just voice-driven interfaces appearing

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:43.090
in a lot of places, which becomes another
way to communicate with an individual.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:47.440
Like, in the beginning, it was email, then
it was notification, then you have mobile

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:53.360
pushes, or pull downs, then you have Windows
tiles on the desktop.

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:56.460
And it becomes more and more natural, like
it’s voice-driven.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:00.320
And if it’s voice-driven, then that, it
is becoming a real dialogue.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:04.550
So, once you have that interface that you
can leverage to build up your application,

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:07.880
how are you thinking about your optimizations?

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.050
If you’re thinking them still in the logistic
regression work, it’s not going to work.

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:13.050
-What does work?

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:17.440
-Well, we’ve taken some early
steps a couple of years ago to look more around

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.800
the quadratic constraints and quadratic programming.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:25.520
It worked for us, that first item, across
multiple different types of dialogues that

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:29.970
we’re having with members, and they work
at, let’s say within a couple of steps.

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:35.130
Overall, where that is all going is still
research.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:38.300
Just one or two steps ahead.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:39.300
-Right.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:40.920
-And we’re making good progress.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:45.220
And it’s going to actually be very, very, interesting
to see how those things are actually shifting.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:46.020
-Yeah.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:51.380
Well, it’s an exciting time with so much
research in machine learning, and people trying

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:57.060
things, to see how it impacts both the technology
and the people that they’re working with.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:03.710
So, you said at one point that machine learning
actually helps transform, not just inform,

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:05.569
but transform your interactions with your
members.

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:07.180
Is that what you’re talking about here?

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:11.030
-Fundamentally, at the core of
everything, you want an interaction to be

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:12.760
very natural.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:16.800
Whether the design is superb, and it fits
exactly the way that you would anticipate it,

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.830
it just feels natural.

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:21.410
And that’s exactly where our field comes
into play.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:22.770
How do you make that thing natural?

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:26.340
It’s not just a design perspective, but
it’s also, what is the content that you’re showing?

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:27.400
How are you going there?

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:30.340
So, it’s transforming that experience, instead
of just making it better.

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:31.350
-Right.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:32.980
So, I’m a LinkedIn member myself.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:34.430
-Thank you.

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:35.430
-Yeah.

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:42.760
And I got to tell you, I have noticed, over
the last year, a difference in how I’m getting notified.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:47.680
And one thing I’ve noticed is that they
seem more personal to me?

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:52.750
Like, I would get a notification about a person
that I know, or care about, as opposed to

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.940
just feeling like you’re trying to pull
me into the app, right?

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:01.210
-So, that’s exactly what we
worked over the last, I would say two years,

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:06.210
to make it exactly that, compared to me pinging
you, “Hey, come back, come back, come back.”

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:09.100
I don't know why you would come back, 
but here it is.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.400
Here’s like 2,000 reasons for you to come
back, pick one…

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:17.460
To more, hey, here’s something of value
within the context of your interest of LinkedIn,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.260
or your value propositions we believe are
important.

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:24.500
Here’s something that hey, on one side,
you ought to know, it would be a good thing,

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:27.300
and on the other hand, it’s to share within
the network also.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:28.300
-Right.

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:32.340
So, I have to say, that makes me happy that
the kinds of technologies you’re working on,

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:37.180
especially machine-learning technologies
that might be helping to broaden your understanding

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:43.700
of what I like, what I’m interested in,
is actually playing out in the real world

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:44.820
for people like me.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:49.080
And I tend to be skeptical and cynical of,
you know, high-tech notifications.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:51.900
I don’t like those red notifications on
my phone, but anyway…

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:52.640
-Sure.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.760
It’s kind of interesting when you pick up
the red thing.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:56.370
We associated red with danger.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:59.220
And it never occurred to me
why would it be red.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.470
Maybe it’s saying like, oh, you need to
do something like right now.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.300
But we have so many of them.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.780
And that’s why I think we’re ready for
that next iteration, that other change.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:11.220
I look at my mailbox, and it has like
numbers in the hundreds.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:12.750
From time to time I clear it out.

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:17.230
I look at my phone, and it’s interesting
to see how some interfaces have decided to

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:19.960
keep the number low, and others to keep the
number high.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:24.240
And to me, it seems that the ones that keep
the number high is those that are still screaming

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.340
at you, and the ones that keep the number
low is to say like, we understand that you

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:28.760
have a busy life.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.380
We understand that you have a lot of things
and a lot of applications on your phone.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.760
Let’s make sure that if I put a number out
there, it’s something that would be really

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:39.960
good for you to know, compared to just,
“Come back, come back, come back!”

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.880
-And that’s a fine line, right?

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:47.440
Because you’re competing for people’s
attention in an increasingly crowded world.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.870
And where do you, you know, stop ALL CAPS,
and whisper to…?

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.350
-But it seems to be working,
right?

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:55.350
-Yeah, for me….

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:56.690
-No, but we can see it also.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:01.350
Of course, everybody is, to some extent, informed
by the metrics of the product performances.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:02.350
And we see that change.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:03.350
-Yeah.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:06.400
-Originally, people would be
confused about, “Hey, why LinkedIn?”

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:07.840
And now it becomes more obvious.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:11.900
And then it becomes more natural, because
you actually steer the conversation to the

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:12.900
right places.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:13.900
-Right.

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:14.940
Which is interesting.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:20.080
This whole topic of conversation that we’re
on right now is talking about how technology

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:25.180
is actually making me feel more personal towards
a particular product.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.290
Which is somewhat, sort of, backwards.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:28.720
But I like it.

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:29.720
It’s working.

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:30.720
Good job.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:36.100
-Data science, the terms themselves,
were not “glued together” to define what

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:39.070
we’re doing less than 7, 8 years ago, 
let’s say.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:40.800
Granted, there are some others that you can,

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.980
that were sort of anticipating
that it was going to go there.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:50.080
But it’s similar with this sense of personalization
or this sense of, it makes sense within the

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.180
scope of what I do, or who it is.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:55.210
And in the past, we used to call it an
augmentation of yourself.

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:56.210
-Oh, yeah.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:59.300
-An agent that works, that extends
and works for you.

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:01.640
And it fits naturally within what you do.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:04.740
And similar to data science back then, people
would look at you blank and say,

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:06.060
“What are you talking about?

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:07.070
What does it mean?”

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:09.710
And today, you can start seeing it.

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:14.480
You say, well, let’s bring all these AI,
let’s bring all this technology to make

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:17.380
me quote-unquote better.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:20.080
Better as an understanding of what’s happening
around me.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:24.070
Better in the way that I can connect, interact
with it, augment the way that I can deal with

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:28.560
the problems that I need to do at work, and
make me better at that.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:35.720
[music]

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:39.320
-Most of the researchers that I’ve
talked to on this podcast are big

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.120
“open source” fans, and I think you are too.

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:45.780
You’ve published articles, given talks,
and you’re on the open source council, or

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:48.050
you oversee the open source council at LinkedIn.

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:51.220
Why is open source a good thing, especially
for researchers?

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:55.211
-Well, I started by saying that
I love science because we all stand on the

00:21:55.211 --> 00:21:57.000
shoulder of giants.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.640
But open source is certainly a movement that
has just accelerated.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:04.820
And I have to say that in the beginning that,
everybody was wondering, is it going to stick

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:05.820
for real?

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:07.330
Is it going to stay forever?

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:11.980
But it just fit so naturally, the way that
we develop things as developers.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:16.610
If you look at any company that has more than
two people, eventually there’s some level

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:21.150
of abstraction that have built, and people
just leverage the code of each other.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:25.210
And open source, it’s just a further extension
to reaching out to the entire community.

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:31.080
I was always a big proponent, even prior to
starting at LinkedIn, because we were just

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.230
leveraging solutions, and every other individuals
had developed.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:39.210
For example, at LinkedIn, the search engine,
originally, the core of it was Lucene, an

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:40.910
Apache open source project.

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:45.820
Then we felt like, well, since we’re riding
on those giants, we should contribute back.

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:49.860
And some other individuals will create businesses
around it, and that creates a whole ecosystem

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.730
that becomes that much, much better.

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:57.370
Then through it, we stumbled upon truths,
some of them being that you actually write

00:22:57.370 --> 00:23:01.280
a much better code when you actually think
about open sourcing it, compared to

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.340
when it’s kept internally.

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:07.440
And not only do you write better code, but
you have a better way to reason about it.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.720
You look at it, does it make sense, does it
not make sense?

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:13.400
Does it complement what is out there, or does
it not complement what is out there?

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.280
And a lot of goodness comes through that.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:23.230
-So, when we talked before, you mentioned
open source code as similar to a peer-reviewed

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:26.570
research paper that you’re publishing in
some way.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.200
Can you explain a little bit about what you
think about that?

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:31.980
-I usually take two allegories
about open source.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:37.320
One, is it feels like peer review, because
in a sense, you’re writing a piece of software,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.710
you’re documenting it, and you’re pushing
it out there.

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:42.100
But there’s so many publications.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:45.780
And if your paper is not good, nobody is going
to actually build other things from it.

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:47.520
You’re not going to be cited much.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:49.330
And open source is the same.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:52.320
If you’re not, investing into your product
to make it great, nobody’s going to actually

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:53.320
really leverage from it.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:58.200
And I used to take another one, which is I
guess through my life experience.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.790
Open sourcing is a little baby also.

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:02.320
You cherish it.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:03.320
It grows.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:06.640
But then at some point of time, you’ve got
to let it have its own life.

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:10.120
And it’s like your kid that grew up and
now becomes mature.

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:14.200
And they’re going to do something that you
may not have envisioned, which is perfect.

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.460
And open sourcing is the same.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.010
You sometimes have the tendency to keep that
kid at home.

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:22.350
I’d say, “Well, no, let them discover
the rest of the world.”

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.850
-Igor, I just open sourced my daughter
at the University of Washington.

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:27.600
-[laughs] Congratulations.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.120
-I totally get what you mean. [laughs]

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:36.500
Let’s talk about LinkedIn’s relationship
with the larger academic and research community

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.070
through public/private research partnerships.

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:42.000
You have a program called The Economic Graph.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.090
And it started I think as a challenge, but
now it’s an ongoing program.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:49.900
Give us an overview of what it is and how
it came about, and what research you focus on.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.500
-So, the Economic Graph challenge.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:58.870
The idea of letting external individuals from
LinkedIn look at our data and answer some

00:24:58.870 --> 00:25:03.691
extremely pertinent and relevant questions,
was there from the time that I joined LinkedIn,

00:25:03.691 --> 00:25:05.800
and probably even before that.

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:11.480
So, how do we create an environment where
we preserve that privacy of the individual,

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.700
and open it up to the community?

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:15.940
That was what the Economic Graph challenge was.

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:21.120
We basically reached out to the, to global
community, friends and family, and others,

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:25.320
to say, “Well, if you had LinkedIn’s data,
what are the questions that you would want

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:26.550
to answer?”

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:31.190
And within that, we had more than 200 proposals
that came back.

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:33.470
We selected, uh, 12.

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.670
The ideas were just brilliant.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:39.870
They ranged from, how do you think about micro
industries and different environments from

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:46.150
a geo perspective, like the fashion industry
in Milan, or, let’s say, the rise of electric

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:54.400
cars in the valley, for example, to, how do
women define themselves, or write about themselves

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:58.740
on LinkedIn compared to men, if you control
for multiple factors?

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:02.610
But again, the resources are limited, so you
reach out to the community to go....

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:07.561
-So, the people that participate in this
with you, the academics or researchers, come

00:26:07.561 --> 00:26:13.559
from all different walks of the research life,
and they participate by presenting a proposal

00:26:13.559 --> 00:26:17.010
and getting access to some of the data from LinkedIn?

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.170
Is that how that works?

00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:24.040
-Well, originally, we, as I mentioned, we just
broad-casted and we got a lot of proposals back.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:28.220
And then we would evaluate on the idea, the
interestingness of the idea, but also the

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:30.860
ability to execute on that idea.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:34.520
Meaning that you have the ability to, using
our data, answer the question.

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:35.520
-Right.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:38.740
-Most ended up by being from
academia.

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:41.000
-What’s your overall goal with Economic Graph?

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:45.120
To create economic opportunity
for every member of the global workforce.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:49.040
And that’s where I got to believe a bit
more into the vision and mission of LinkedIn,

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:50.850
to create that economic opportunity.

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:54.140
And not only “an” economic opportunity,
but the right one for you;

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:55.840
the one that you aspire to.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:27:00.360
So, the way that we used the Economic Graph,
on one side, it still needs to be descriptive,

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:03.000
because it provides the value 
of the labor market.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:06.660
It provides an image, whether it’s instantaneous,
or whether it’s in the past.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:10.720
It provides a sense of, “What are people
doing in the labor market, and what are the

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:12.020
movements within it?”

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.800
It’s a living entity, and 
we provide a picture of it.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:18.440
And there’s tremendous value to it, because
the timelines are very quick.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:23.350
You don’t have to wait 6 months, or 3 months,
to get a government statistic, but it provides

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:25.590
a good picture, so then that
is extremely valuable.

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:26.590
-Yeah.

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:31.660
-The other is, to understand
and see that careers are also living,

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:34.880
they migrate, they shift from one domain to another.

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:37.700
You have multiple careers in your lifetime.

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:40.690
And understanding what it takes and what it
doesn’t take, and helping all members to

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:41.820
actually build up their careers.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:46.040
So, that’s where the Economic Graph is also
moving, to figure it out, how do I share that

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.100
information with you?

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:48.929
How do I help you nurture your career?

00:27:48.929 --> 00:27:54.870
And in the end, well, that’s to create economic
opportunity and get you along your life cycle.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:28:00.100
Everybody, I believe, starts a job thinking
that it’s going to be the job they’re

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:01.390
going to do forever.

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:05.040
I started LinkedIn thinking that, “Ehh,
2 to 3 years of my life,” and 10 years later,

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:06.660
I’m still here.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:10.140
But… and actually, I’m still here because
of the vision and the belief of the ability

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:11.650
that we can move it forward.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:15.770
But once you think in that route, you’re
seeing the fluidity of the labor market as

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:19.140
being something that is important.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:22.840
And I wasn’t an economist before coming
to LinkedIn, whatsoever.

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:28.800
But there’s some very fascinating mind-frame
around it, or frameworks to think about it.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:37.540
[music]

00:28:37.540 --> 00:28:38.820
-You’re a researcher.

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:44.220
You’ve ended up parlaying a research interest
into a career.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:47.820
What advice would you give researchers who
were heading into the field of maybe data science,

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:51.260
or even more generally, as they prepare
for their next steps after grad school?

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:53.480
-The main one, be curious.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:54.800
Continue to be curious.

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:58.610
There’s a sense of, if you went into getting
a PhD, you’re wanting to solve a problem,

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:03.350
there’s something that was missing, you
went at it, and you dig in, and you attempted

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:07.809
to move the answer a bit further along…
that’s great.

00:29:07.809 --> 00:29:10.530
It’s certainly one of the best experiences.

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:16.380
On the other hand, make sure that once that
happens, don’t just limit the focus.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:21.620
I think that’s, at times, what researchers
are missing, that, yes, it’s valuable to

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:27.610
dig in, very much, but be aware of the rest,
and make connections from that to maybe better

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:30.690
what you’re doing, see different paradigms,
different perspectives.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:37.270
I was listening to Fabiola Gianotti just recently,
who is the head of the CERN.

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:41.240
She studied first humanities before moving
into physics.

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.920
And I view myself as a scientist also.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:50.840
And the things that I value more, that I wish
I had more going to school, is actually humanities.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:54.460
Not math or the rest, because I feel like,
no, I get these anyway.

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:55.900
So, that’s a given.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:57.720
But the rest if missing.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:01.720
And if you think about it this way, today
at LinkedIn, I’m trying to understand, from

00:30:01.721 --> 00:30:06.120
a member’s perspective, what are the struggles
that they’re going through in order to make

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.260
their career better?

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:12.420
And you get that more by this site exposure,
by this interest.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:13.420
Make these connections.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:16.230
And these connections have to be outside of
just your domain.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:20.520
If I had one encouragement, it’s keep the
wonder. Just go for it.

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:23.420
Just keep the wonder and be willing to learn
different topics.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:25.460
And challenge yourself a lot.

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:28.320
-Igor Perisic, thank you so much.

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:30.660
It’s been a delight to talk to you today.

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.220
-You're welcome very much.
Thank you.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:37.120
[music]

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:41.440
